# Reproducible research baseline

## Snapshot identity

This baseline was fetched from `origin/main` on 2026-10-13. The exact repository
snapshot is:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Commit | `ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc` |
| Git tree | `bd5b3835d57cad67d387c7bb92687e88bf48f982` |
| Parent | `5d93ac0eec111ee56e07548f55085fd553b9e4f5` |
| Commit date | `2026-10-10T06:45:40-05:00` |
| Theorem state | `2026-10-10.audit.234` |
| Manifest SHA-256 | `a8b571cacc2c77623a991f50114a70141d59bcde9685f916db8153599e58b2b6` |

At this snapshot, Level I remains open. Target A is open through Section 177, Target B
is open at the global shell-stability inequality, and Target C-I is closed while C-II
remains open. See [`../STATE.md`](../STATE.md) and the target route maps for the exact
claims and hypotheses. This baseline does not certify those proofs.

The ledger contains 451 unique theorem IDs: 372 canonical, 3 audited-working, 62
regression, 5 superseded, and 9 rejected. Its registered theorem-ID graph has 451 nodes
and 845 `depends_on` edges. The manifest registers 94 unique certificate scripts across
130 entries. The generated artifact index contains 1,410 records. These are inventory
counts, not measures of proof completeness. Registered theorem IDs are distributed as
0 for Target A, 1 for Target B, and 450 for Target C; Target A's active authority is
route/module-based here, so the ID graph must not be read as a complete theorem or proof
dependency inventory.

## Reproduce the checks

Use a clean worktree at the recorded commit. The validation utility may be taken from a
checkout that contains this document; `--root` points it at the exact historical tree.

```sh
git clone https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99.git e-99
cd e-99
git fetch origin main
git worktree add --detach /tmp/e99-baseline-2026-10-10 ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc
python3 -m venv /tmp/e99-baseline-venv
/tmp/e99-baseline-venv/bin/python -m pip install -r /tmp/e99-baseline-2026-10-10/tools/requirements-theorem-state.txt
/tmp/e99-baseline-venv/bin/python tools/verify_research_baseline.py \
  --root /tmp/e99-baseline-2026-10-10 \
  --expected-commit ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc
```

The utility refuses a wrong commit or a dirty checkout, prints the manifest summary and
identity-file hashes, then runs the theorem-state checker, its CI unit suite, the active
route summary checker, the push-mode state/PR checker, the repository's research unit
tests, all registered certificates, and `git diff --check`. A failed command makes the
overall result fail; the tool still reports later independent checks. Dependencies come
from `tools/requirements-theorem-state.txt`.

## Recorded validation

The checks were run in an isolated Python 3.14.7 virtual environment with PyYAML 6.0.3,
jsonschema 4.26.0, NumPy 2.5.4, SciPy 1.18.1, SymPy 1.14.0, and mpmath 1.3.0.

| Check | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `tools/check_theorem_state.py` | Passed; manifest schema, registered dependency edges, generated state views, artifact metadata, and lifecycle constraints were mechanically consistent. |
| The three theorem-state CI test modules | Passed: 22 tests. |
| `tools/check_active_route_summaries.py` | Passed. |
| Push-mode `tools/check_theorem_state_pr.py` | Passed. |
| Research unit-test modules under `research/` | Passed: 65 tests across 11 files. These are not run by the current theorem-state workflow. |
| Registered certificates | Passed: 94 unique certificates. |
| `git diff --check` on the clean baseline | Passed. |

The end-to-end `verify_research_baseline.py` run against a second clean worktree at this
commit completed **7/7 checks**. The certificate step took 325.9 seconds in this local
environment; future runtime depends on hardware and package versions.

This was a local run, not a GitHub Actions run. The requirements file specifies compatible
version ranges rather than a hash-locked environment, and the workflow selects Python
`3.x`; exact long-term environment reproduction therefore requires recording or pinning
the resolved toolchain separately.

